    <entry>
        <title type="html"><![CDATA[Ask HN: If the models are so good, why doesn't OpenAI just start 100 companies?]]></title>
        <id>link-d2d147873d73c857a64872018464431a</id>
        <link href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50200997"/>
        <updated>2026-10-24T16:14:11.928Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<div class="Story_container"><div class="Story_data"><div class="Story_title"><a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50200997"><span>Ask HN: If the models are so good, why doesn't OpenAI just start 100 companies?</span></a></div><div class="Story_meta"><span><a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50200997">680 points</a></span><span class="Story_separator">|</span><span><a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=ex-aws-dude"><span>ex-aws-dude</span></a></span><span class="Story_separator">|</span><span><a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50200997">6 days ago</a></span><span class="Story_separator">|</span><span><a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50200997">753 comments</a></span><div class="Story_comment"><span>Title is a bit of an exaggeration but the question is more: if the frontier models are so good, why even sell them as your primary money maker?<p>Couldn't OpenAI or Anthropic make a lot more money by just starting companies themselves that leverage the models and building their own products?</p><p>Is selling tokens really the best strategy?</p><p>Especially with commodification and prices declining wouldn't that indicate there is more money to be made on the application-side of the models rather than the utility side</p></span></div></div></div></div>]]></content>
    </entry>
